{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And he saw.</b> The angel." "<b>That he could not prevail over him.</b> Over Jacob — that is, he did not have permission to harm him; rather, the Holy One, blessed be He, sent him to strengthen him and bless him. Even so, he harmed him slightly, to make known that he was an angel. And many instances of the language of יכולת (ability) like this we find in Scripture: לא יוכל העם לעלות אל ה׳, and ולא יכול להלחם עליה דישעיה. That is, the ability is not in his hand." "<b>And he struck.</b> The angel." "<b>In the hollow of his thigh.</b> Of Jacob. He wanted to raise Jacob's thigh, so that perhaps he would fall." "<b>And it was dislocated.</b> אתק״א in Old French, for there are words whose interpretation reverses between two forms — active and passive — such as this one. And likewise פן תקע נפשי ממך, and we find ויעקב תקע את אהלו בהר, and likewise ועשית סירותיו לדשנו והרים את הדשן, and ותשרש שרשיה ושרשך מארץ חיים." "<b>The hollow of Jacob's thigh.</b> Even though HaShem promised him 'I will guard you wherever you go,' the angel harmed him, because he feared Esau, as it is written: 'and Jacob was greatly afraid.' And so we find with Moses: even though HaShem said to him 'I will be with you,' the angel harmed him on the road at the inn, because he feared Pharaoh, as it is written: 'send, I pray, by the hand of him whom You will send.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {4 "The Old French forms אירי״ש and אתק״א are left in transliteration as they appear; they likely represent Old French equivalents for the Hebrew root תקע in its active and passive forms."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:07:15.977165918Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}